WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.364
The Cargo.toml manifest is the project's
declaration of intent.

2
00:00:03.444 --> 00:00:06.932
It describes the package, dependencies,
features, workspace relationships,

3
00:00:07.012 --> 00:00:09.824
build settings, metadata, and sometimes
publishing behavior.

4
00:00:09.904 --> 00:00:13.236
For security review, the manifest answers
a simple but important question:

5
00:00:13.316 --> 00:00:16.700
what did this project ask Cargo to include
and how is that request configured?

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.654
Cargo.lock answers a different question.

7
00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:21.130
It records the resolved versions selected
for a build.

8
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.802
That matters because dependency
requirements in the manifest can allow a range of

9
00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:28.423
versions, while the lockfile captures the
concrete dependency graph that Cargo

10
00:00:28.503 --> 00:00:29.388
actually selected.

11
00:00:29.468 --> 00:00:30.665
The manifest shows intent.

12
00:00:30.745 --> 00:00:32.151
The lockfile shows resolution.

13
00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:35.303
Direct dependencies are the crates the
project requests explicitly.

14
00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:37.830
Transitive dependencies arrive through
those crates.

15
00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.346
Both are security-relevant because both
become part of the trusted codebase.

16
00:00:41.426 --> 00:00:44.967
A project may have only a few direct
dependencies and still pull in a larger tree of

17
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:48.431
parsing libraries, platform helpers, build
utilities, procedural macros,

18
00:00:48.511 --> 00:00:49.709
or optional integrations.

19
00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.433
Version requirements deserve review
because they shape what Cargo can resolve now

20
00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:54.711
and during future updates.

21
00:00:54.791 --> 00:00:58.592
Flexible requirements may ease
maintenance, but they still need release discipline.

22
00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:00.910
Very narrow requirements may preserve
stability,

23
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.761
but they can also slow security updates.

24
00:01:02.841 --> 00:01:05.548
The right choice depends on the project,
the release process,

25
00:01:05.628 --> 00:01:07.346
and the team's ability to test changes.

26
00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:11.175
Workspace dependency visibility is
especially important in larger Rust codebases.

27
00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:14.014
Shared dependencies, feature unification,
internal crates,

28
00:01:14.094 --> 00:01:17.218
and multiple binaries can make it harder
to see what each artifact uses.

29
00:01:17.298 --> 00:01:20.995
Defensive teams review Cargo.toml and
Cargo.lock together in code review and CI/CD

30
00:01:21.075 --> 00:01:22.689
so unknown, unnecessary, outdated,

31
00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:25.060
or surprising dependencies do not enter
unnoticed.
